  Current coverage:

   Capability                            Extended implementation
  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
   Topology-derived candidate tunnels    Implemented with bounded DFS, path costs, and caching
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Piecewise bandwidth policy            Implemented as marginal weighted segments, but not exact B4 fair-share functions
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   More scalable allocation              Incremental resource-closure recomputation exists, but the core optimizer remains greedy
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Global fairness-aware quantization    Not implemented; still uses local largest-remainder rounding
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Source-edge rate limiter              Simulated through aggregate policer output
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Topology and link failures            Modeled through trunks, physical members, topology versions, and headroom
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Admission control                     Not implemented
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Traffic Engineering Database          Only approximated by the in-memory controller state
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Transactional deployment              Simulated with monotonically increasing versions
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Dependency ordering                   Described in the README but not implemented
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Acknowledgement and retry             Not implemented
  ────────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   OpenFlow programming                  Represented by printed groups and policers, not actual southbound operations

  The next useful extension should therefore focus on the remaining gaps:

  1. Make flow groups B4-compatible: {source, destination, QoS}, with multiple applications composed into one flow group.
  2. Represent each application policy as bandwidth = f(fair_share) and compose those functions.
  3. Add an admission stage that rejects unreachable traffic and traffic whose minimum guarantee cannot be satisfied.
  4. Replace independent rounding with a global quantizer that checks every trunk after each bucket assignment.
  5. Add a Traffic Engineering Database containing desired, staged, and installed policies.
  6. Build an explicit deployment dependency graph:

  tunnels
     ↓
  tunnel groups
     ↓
  source policers
     ↓
  classifiers
     ↓
  activate policy version

  7. Simulate per-device prepare/commit acknowledgements, retry, idempotency, rollback, and old-version cleanup.
  8. Separate discovered topology from configured policy, so failures update discovered state and trigger recomputation.
  9. Keep the actual device operation behind an interface, allowing a simulator now and an OpenFlow-like backend later.

  I recommend creating max_min_te_b4_controller.cpp as the next stage:

  max_min_te_completed.cpp
      Minimal fairness and multipath demonstration

  max_min_te_extended.cpp
      Topology, aggregation, caching, incremental recomputation,
      headroom, policers, and simulated transactions

  max_min_te_b4_controller.cpp
      B4-compatible bandwidth functions, admission control,
      global quantization, TED, dependency-aware deployment,
      acknowledgements, retries, and rollback

  This preserves the educational progression while avoiding duplicate implementations. 
  The remaining work is substantial enough that adding it directly to max_min_te_completed.cpp would make the
  “completed” example harder to understand and still not produce a faithful B4 controller.
